WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.874
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.174 --> 00:00:08.549
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:15.402
Unit fifteen: Data Analytics. Today you will explain dependence with depends on + noun / noun clause.

4
00:00:15.702 --> 00:00:18.122
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:22.311
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:24.581
Data analytics.

7
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:30.706
Data analytics means examining data to find patterns and support decisions.

8
00:00:31.317 --> 00:00:34.907
Data analytics revealed which branches were losing customers.

9
00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:38.378
Your turn. Say: Data analytics.

10
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:42.827
Data analytics.

11
00:00:43.583 --> 00:00:45.293
Key performance indicator.

12
00:00:46.283 --> 00:00:51.081
Key performance indicator means a measurable value showing progress toward a goal.

13
00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:54.844
Delivery time is our main key performance indicator.

14
00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:59.008
Your turn. Say: Key performance indicator.

15
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:03.483
Key performance indicator.

16
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:05.311
Dashboard.

17
00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:09.988
Dashboard means a visual display of key data in one place.

18
00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:13.709
The manager checks the sales dashboard every morning.

19
00:01:14.317 --> 00:01:16.957
Your turn. Say: Dashboard.

20
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.010
Dashboard.

21
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.030
Predictive model.

22
00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:28.199
Predictive model means a statistical tool that forecasts future outcomes.

23
00:01:28.783 --> 00:01:31.886
The predictive model flags customers likely to leave.

24
00:01:32.483 --> 00:01:35.449
Your turn. Say: Predictive model.

25
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.673
Predictive model.

26
00:01:40.417 --> 00:01:41.952
Data-driven decision.

27
00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:47.272
Data-driven decision means a choice based on analysis rather than instinct.

28
00:01:47.883 --> 00:01:50.370
Closing the branch was a data-driven decision.

29
00:01:50.983 --> 00:01:54.123
Your turn. Say: Data-driven decision.

30
00:01:56.967 --> 00:01:58.490
Data-driven decision.

31
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:00.472
Data quality.

32
00:02:01.483 --> 00:02:05.457
Data quality means how accurate, complete and reliable data is.

33
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.781
Poor data quality produced misleading reports.

34
00:02:09.383 --> 00:02:12.256
Your turn. Say: Data quality.

35
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.229
Data quality.

36
00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:18.019
Insight.

37
00:02:19.017 --> 00:02:22.851
Insight means a deep, useful understanding gained from analysis.

38
00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.994
The key insight was that most sales happen after 9 p.m.

39
00:02:27.583 --> 00:02:29.911
Your turn. Say: Insight.

40
00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:33.779
Insight.

41
00:02:34.517 --> 00:02:35.924
Visualization.

42
00:02:36.917 --> 00:02:40.484
Visualization means presenting data as charts or graphics.

43
00:02:41.083 --> 00:02:44.023
A clear visualization convinced the board in seconds.

44
00:02:44.617 --> 00:02:47.327
Your turn. Say: Visualization.

45
00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:51.400
Visualization.

46
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.804
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:04.390
Visal, the supermarket's new predictive model said sales of ice would fall in April. Instead, they doubled. What went wrong?

48
00:03:05.483 --> 00:03:15.299
The model was fine, but good insight depends heavily on data quality. Half the stores had entered their numbers in the wrong units.

49
00:03:16.383 --> 00:03:18.894
So the analysis was based on faulty data?

50
00:03:19.983 --> 00:03:27.164
Exactly. Whether a forecast is useful depends on how carefully the data was collected and cleaned.

51
00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.215
How can they prevent this next time?

52
00:03:31.317 --> 00:03:41.225
Set clear standards for data entry and check outliers. Success depends less on clever algorithms than on reliable inputs.

53
00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:44.018
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:44.883 --> 00:03:48.241
Grammar focus: Depends on plus noun clause.

55
00:03:48.541 --> 00:03:49.763
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:53.413
Good insight depends heavily on data quality.

57
00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:57.895
The forecast's value depends on how carefully the data was cleaned.

58
00:03:58.783 --> 00:04:02.408
Success depends less on algorithms than on reliable inputs.

59
00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:14.564
After 'depend on', a wh- clause uses statement word order: depends on how the data was collected (not 'how was the data collected'). 'Depending on' can start a phrase: Depending on the data, …

60
00:04:15.317 --> 00:04:19.327
Be careful! Don't say: Insight depends of data quality.

61
00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:23.765
Say: Insight depends on data quality.

62
00:04:24.065 --> 00:04:25.113
Depend on.

63
00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.866
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:31.341
Garbage In, Garbage Out.

65
00:04:31.641 --> 00:04:36.880
Data analytics transforms raw numbers into insights that support better business decisions.

66
00:04:37.867 --> 00:04:44.661
However, the value of any analysis depends entirely on whether the underlying data is accurate, complete and relevant.

67
00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:51.834
Analysts often say 'garbage in, garbage out', meaning sophisticated models cannot rescue flawed inputs.

68
00:04:52.833 --> 00:04:59.756
Dashboards and visualizations make patterns visible to managers, but they can also give false confidence when the data behind them is weak.

69
00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:07.074
Organisations that invest in data quality before investing in advanced tools usually see far greater returns.

70
00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:12.076
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:12.917 --> 00:05:15.624
The result depends blank the quality of the sample.

72
00:05:15.924 --> 00:05:18.475
A, of. B, on. C, from.

73
00:05:22.033 --> 00:05:23.557
The answer is B: on.

74
00:05:24.717 --> 00:05:28.086
A measurable value showing progress toward a goal is a blank.

75
00:05:28.386 --> 00:05:32.887
A, key performance indicator. B, visualization. C, dashboard.

76
00:05:36.433 --> 00:05:39.234
The answer is A: key performance indicator.

77
00:05:40.383 --> 00:05:43.277
How accurate and reliable data is, is its blank.

78
00:05:43.577 --> 00:05:47.846
A, insight. B, predictive model. C, data quality.

79
00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:53.458
The answer is C: data quality.

80
00:05:54.617 --> 00:05:56.372
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:56.672 --> 00:06:04.111
A, It depends on how much time do we have.. B, It depends on how much time we have.. C, It depends how much time we have on..

82
00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:11.384
The answer is B: It depends on how much time we have..

83
00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:16.314
According to the reading, 'garbage in, garbage out' means…

84
00:06:16.614 --> 00:06:23.042
A, models cannot rescue flawed inputs. B, data should be deleted. C, dashboards are useless.

85
00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:30.283
The answer is A: models cannot rescue flawed inputs.

86
00:06:31.467 --> 00:06:34.479
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:35.317 --> 00:06:38.733
Why is 'data quality' as important as the analysis itself?

88
00:06:55.517 --> 00:06:56.959
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:57.259 --> 00:07:33.321
Data quality is as important as analysis because even the most advanced model will produce wrong answers if its inputs are inaccurate. Every conclusion depends on whether the numbers truly reflect reality. If a hospital records ages incorrectly, a model could set the wrong treatment priorities. Poor data is dangerous because charts look convincing regardless. Therefore, I believe organisations should check, clean and document their data before trusting any analysis built on it.

90
00:07:33.621 --> 00:07:35.330
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:36.217 --> 00:07:40.970
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and depends on plus noun clause.

92
00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:45.850
Today's value is analytical rigor. Test the numbers before you believe the story.

93
00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:48.709
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:49.009 --> 00:07:52.312
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

